            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Your house has an air leakage of 5.6 ACH @ 50 Pa; this is relatively high but indicates there is plenty of opportunity for energy savings. 

The following is a list of locations where air leakage was observed:

-electrical outlets on exterior walls

-electrical and pipe penetrations

-baseboard trims

-door weatherstripping

-basement header (above basement door)



For larger gaps such as around wires and pipes you can use spray foam insulation. It comes in a can will expand to fill large gaps around an inch or more. Anything smaller than a Â¼Â or so can be sealed with caulking, such as along baseboards.



The basement door closes tight but there is still some air leakage happening. You could look for thicker weatherstripping material so that when the door latches closed, it contacts the weatherstripping all the way around.



The floor above the basement door was noted to be always cold and the thermal camera showed that space between the floors to be cold as well. There could be a lack of insulation or air barrier, both of which could lead to this area being colder than normal. When I poked my head up into the space from the mechanical room, I couldnÂt see it clear enough to see what the problem was. In any case, likely the best solution would to add some additional polystyrene insulation on top of whatÂs there. This will add insulation and act as an air barrier if you also tape or spray foam around the seams.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 155&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2" (R10) of exterior insulation. I have modelled adding another 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The attic of your house was assumed to have 12 inches (R40) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding another 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 40&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There is no rebate available for adding ceiling insulation when you already have R40.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</ceilings>
                <foundation>Your basement floor is noted as being uninsulated. I have modeled adding 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation to the joist space below the floor. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement floor, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 165&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>A drain-water heat-recovery (DWHR) system provides a simple, maintenance-free way to reclaim some of the heat primarily from shower-water lost down the drain. These systems can save up to 40 per cent on water heating costs.



Here's how it works:  In a conventional system, cold water enters a hot water heater where it is turned into hot water.  When someone showers, the hot water proceeds from the heater to the shower where it is then lost down the drain. In a DWHR system, cold water first runs through copper coils wound tightly around the vertical plumbing drain that serves the shower. Copper is an excellent heat conductor that conducts the warmth of the drain water to the cold water in the coils without mixing the water sources.  As the cold water continues to wind up through the coils around the plumbing drain, it becomes pre-heated.  The pre-heated water continues to the hot water heater. Alternatively, the preheated water can be diverted to the cold-water line that also serves the shower.  The amount of hot water required from the water tank can then be reduced.



DWHR systems should only be installed vertically or they will not perform as intended. Install systems only where there is adequate room on vertical stacks below showers.  



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 42% efficient drain water heat recovery system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate. For a full rebate, the rebate application must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the date of the receipt. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
